<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Feb 20, 2023">
<CPscript title="Random Mapping Trials" level="min" screenmode="map" >
<description>This script follows "DiscreteRMT.xmd" but uses random packings and random triangulations. Experiments like these are quite suggestive: it appears that even randomly generated discrete conformal maps may converge to their classical counterparts as the complexity increases. But, can we prove this?</description>
<text> You should become familiar with "DiscreteRMT.cps" before running this script. We start with the same region G, though you are welcome to use other regions provided in the data section or to generate your own. The difference here is in our packings: we use random packings and random triangulations raher than regular packings.

(Note: the random packings and triangulations used here were generated with 'random_pack' and 'random_tri' calls. These depend on system calls to produce Delaunay triangulations, and these can have issues on some platforms. For this reason and to save computational time, the random objects have been saved as files.) </text>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" iconname="metacontact_offline.png">act 1;Cleanse;Map 0 1;infile_read rand_overlay.p;infile_path kurve.g;disp -g; </cmd>
<text> The first section simply runs our original experiment using a stored random packing as the overlay. In "DiscreteRMT.xmd" we displayed two tools for judging fidelity: the 'g' command imposes a spoke-and-wheel grid, while the 'k' command color codes for quasiconformal distortion. </text>
<Section title="A Prepared Example">
<text> The next command carries out the typical cookie construction with a random overlay packing provided in the data section. Optional commands 'i' and 'd' let you increase or decrease the size of the overlaying packing: you have to do this before proceeding with the rest of the construction. </text>
<cmd tooltip="Overlay with a hexagonal packing" iconname="amarok.png">act 1;Disp -w -c -g; </cmd>
<cmd name="c" mnemonic="c" tooltip="'Cookie' out the packing of Omega" iconname="centrejust.png">act 1;cookie;Disp -w -c -g; </cmd>
<cmd name="m" mnemonic="m" tooltip="Create the max packing in the disc" iconname="psi.png">copy -p1 0;max_pack -p0; Disp -p0 -w -c; </cmd>
<cmd tooltip="Color code boundary circles" iconname="xeyes.png">color -p0 -c s b;put_data -p0 -q1 -cc b;Disp -p0 -w -c -cf b;Disp -p1 -w -c -cf  b -g; </cmd>
<text> RESULT: The mapping between the maximal packing Q in p0 and the circle packing P in p1 is a "discrete conformal mapping" from the unit disc to G. </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Using Random Packings">
<text> The construction here differs from the previous section only in that the overlay will now be randomly generated anew each time. The "complexity" of an experiment is reflected in the number of circles requrested for the random packing. Increasing complexity plays the role of increasing refinement in the earlier experiments.

The complexity is reflected in the variable "N", originally set using our 'colon-equal' notation by 'N:=1000'. You are welcome to edit the command to increase N, but you will also have to increase your patience since computation times go up significantly with N. </text>
<cmd iconname="klines.png">act 1;N:=1000;random_packing _N;geom_to_e;scale 1.1;Disp -w -c -g; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="kghostview.png">[c];[m]; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="network.png">[g]; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="amarok.png">[k] </cmd>
<text> Experiments here are quite convincing: the discrete conformal maps converge to the classical conformal map as complexity increases. There are some caveats, however. Randomness requires some type of "almost surely" statement, since individual experiments can go awry. Also, the randomness can be formulated in different ways. But positive convergence results have been formlated and proven for this type of random construction. </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Using Random Triangulations">
<text> Our second notion of random maps is a major departure from the above and opens a wealth of fascinating questions. The maps defined here will be called "random discrete conformal maps" between the disc and G.

Instead of a random circle packing in G, we will start with a random triangulation T of G. Complexity is set with the number N as before. Roughly 4*sqrt(N) points are distributed randomly along G, roughly N points are distributed randomly inside G using a Poisson Point Process, then the triangulation T of G is created as the "Delaunary" triangulation associated with these points. (I leave it to the user to look up the various terms used here.) 

Watch the process in action. (I 'random_triangulation' fails on your platform, you can replace that call by read in the prepared example 'rand1000.p'.) </text>
<cmd iconname="run.png">act 1;N:=200;random_triangulation -g -N _N;set_vlist -c 0.0 0.0;alpha vlist[0];Disp -w -f -g </cmd>
<cmd iconname="apollon.png">[m] </cmd>
<cmd iconname="userconfig.png">[k] </cmd>
<cmd iconname="metacontact_online.png">[g] </cmd>
<text> What is going on here? Note that there is never a circle packing in G, only a random triangulation T. However, we can compute the maximal packing for T, and that is the circle packing Q in p0 generated by the 'm' command. Q has the same combinatorics as T, so our "map" f: D --> G is may be taken as the piecewise affine map carrying the triangles of the (euclidean) carrier of Q to the corresponding triangles of T. Click the middle mouse button on some triangles to see the link.

When you press 'k' you see that the quasiconformal distortion under f is bad --- we do not see the nice fading red colors we saw in the previous experiment.

On the other hand, press 'g' to see how the grid is carried over. It isn't pretty, but it is doing just about the right thing. Take some screenshots as you do a sequence of experiments with successively greater complexity and see how these behave. Precisely such experiments have led me to the following open conjecture:

CONJECTURE: Random discrete conformal maps from D to G converge in probability in the compact-open topology to the classical conformal map F: D --> G.

There are a lot of buzz words here to work through, but if these experiments capture your interest, perhaps you would find this an area worth investigating. The more intuition we develop the closer we might be to a proof of this conjecture.

Enjoy! </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="i" mnemonic="i" tooltip="increase all radii" iconname="kuickshow.png">adjust_rad -p1 1.2 a;layout -p1;disp -p1 -w -c -g; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="d" mnemonic="d" tooltip="decrease all radii" iconname="mozilla.png">adjust_rad -p1 .9 a;layout -p1;disp -p1 -w -c -g; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="g" mnemonic="g" tooltip="set the grid" iconname="klines.png">set_grid -p0;disp -p0 -w -bc5t3 -u;disp -p1 -w -bc5t3 -g </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="k" mnemonic="k" tooltip="color code for quasiconformal dilatation" iconname="netbeans.png">copy -p0 2;geom_to_e -p2;color -p1 -f qc -q2 -m 1.5 a;disp -p1 -w -ffc -g; </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
